WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.060
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 134, for July 17, 2026, some fascinating

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:14.040
developments to cover today. Astronomers report signs of an atmosphere on a rocky world in the

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:21.460
habitable zone 48 light years away, a first for planets of its kind. Observations indicate the 48

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.660
light-year distant world may retain an atmosphere thick enough to be detected from Earth.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.040
The planet orbits in the conservative habitable zone of its red dwarf host.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.980
Follow-up spectroscopy is planned to determine atmospheric composition.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.880
The detection relies on transit photometry that measures subtle changes in starlight passing

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.620
through any extended gaseous layer. Current data suggests the atmosphere could be denser than

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:54.920
models previously predicted for planets of this size. Additional telescope time has been allocated

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:02.940
to refine the signal. The detection of a possible atmosphere on LHS 1140b stands out because

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:09.820
the planet is both rocky and lies in the conservative habitable zone. Transmission spectroscopy during

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:15.780
transits can reveal absorption features if the atmosphere is thick enough to extend above the surface.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.440
Confirmation would make this world a priority target for future atmospheric studies.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.260
Current models suggest the signal strength is sufficient for detection with existing facilities

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:33.920
if the envelope is sufficiently extended. Repeated transit observations will be needed to build statistical

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:41.020
confidence in the result. What specific gases would you most want to look for first? If Earth were

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:47.880
placed 48 light years away, its atmosphere would span less than 110,000th the width of the star at orbits.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:53.880
Yet that sliver is exactly what astronomers measure. When a planet crosses its star,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.180
a tiny fraction of starlight filters through the upper atmosphere. Molecules there absorb specific

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:06.240
wavelengths imprinting a barcode on the spectrum that reaches our telescopes. The James Webb Space

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.760
Telescope records these changes at precision better than 100 parts per million. Across dozens of

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.360
transits, enough to distinguish between a bare rock and one wrapped in a detectable gaseous envelope.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.740
The surprising number is the scale. The signal from an Earth-sized atmosphere at that distance is

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:32.360
equivalent to blocking an extra 0.01% of the star's light for a few hours. Temperature gradients within

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:38.200
the atmosphere further modulate the depth of absorption lines, revealing whether the gas is heated

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:44.380
from above or below. Yet even with that detection in hand, we still cannot tell whether the atmosphere

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:50.740
is being continuously replenished by volcanic outgassing or is a remnant leftover from formation.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:57.160
The next generation of extremely large ground-based telescopes may resolve this ambiguity by capturing

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:03.940
higher resolution spectra during single transits. Four Raptor engines failed to ignite at T0,

